# V Shots — Runtime configuration (bundled into the app as an asset)
#
# ⚠️ SECURITY NOTE (read before adding anything else here):
# flutter_dotenv's own README is explicit: ".env files are bundled into
# the app binary and can be extracted by anyone with access to the
# build. Use for non-sensitive configuration only."
#
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY below is safe to ship in a client app BY DESIGN —
# it is Supabase's public, RLS-governed key (every table in
# supabase_setup.sql has Row Level Security enabled; the anon key can
# only do what those RLS policies explicitly allow, regardless of who
# has it). This is the same key Supabase's own docs put directly in
# client-side JS.
#
# NEVER put these in this file: the database password, a Supabase
# "service_role" key, GitHub tokens, or any other credential that
# bypasses RLS or grants admin access. Those belong in GitHub Actions
# Secrets (server-side/CI only) or a real secrets manager — never in
# an asset bundled into the shipped app.

SUPABASE_URL=https://jzxtxqjheggyoqwohqjg.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.eyJpc3MiOiJzdXBhYmFzZSIsInJlZiI6Imp6eHR4cWpoZWdneW9xd29ocWpnIiwicm9sZSI6ImFub24iLCJpYXQiOjE3ODYxODM4OTcsImV4cCI6MjEwMTc1OTg5N30.fD6pKQ4VRG-AoF-nLdpU9iMK1qWz4N-diqMUOJESVw8

# Web Client ID for Google Sign-In. Per Supabase's own docs, this MUST
# be the Web-type OAuth client (not the Android client) — Supabase's
# auth server verifies the Google ID token's audience against this ID.
# See lib/core/backend/auth_service.dart for details.
#
# 🔴 OPEN ITEM: the Client ID provided
# (1025304697342-1egjkv6vdauklm44r0gep0ptiqovqkmt.apps.googleusercontent.com)
# was listed simply as "Google OAuth Client ID" without specifying which
# OAuth client TYPE it is (Web vs Android). It is used below as-is, but
# if Google Sign-In fails with DEVELOPER_ERROR or an audience-mismatch
# error from Supabase, the fix is: go to Google Cloud Console →
# Credentials, create/locate a client of type "Web application"
# specifically, and put THAT ID here instead. The Android-type client
# (auto-matched by package name + SHA-1) is never referenced directly
# in app code — Android's Google Play Services finds it automatically
# via the SHA-1 fingerprints already registered.
GOOGLE_WEB_CLIENT_ID=1025304697342-1egjkv6vdauklm44r0gep0ptiqovqkmt.apps.googleusercontent.com
